-  "novel_mechanics_found": "lf52 L3+ is multi-screen rail-carrier PEG SOLITAIRE with a built-in move oracle: ACTION6 on a peg paints a color-3 selection ring plus color-2 markers on every LEGAL landing square (pegs with no legal moves give a completely silent click) -- the game enumerates its own moves, eliminating guesswork. Jumps are two-cell hops over an adjacent peg (crossed peg removed). Carriers (bccccb sprites) are mobile sockets on rails driven by ACTION1-4, and ALL carriers move simultaneously on each press (each in whatever direction it can); a peg jumps INTO an aligned carrier socket over a crossed peg, rides it across rooms/screens (camera follows), and jumps out the same way -- the L2 registry mechanic generalized to a network of rails, shafts, and inverted-U ducts. The world is larger than one screen and scrolls. L4 introduces TORCHES (color-15 boxes with color-7 cores): crossable-but-indestructible jump obstacles that let a SOLO peg travel (hop over a torch, torch survives) -- breaking the classic solitaire rule that lone pegs are stuck. Stranding detection: if no legal moves remain with >1 peg, all pegs recolor (fill 14 -> 2) and the board auto-resets to the level start (unlimited retries, no timer). The win condition is global reduce-to-ONE-peg.",
-  "narrative": "Resolved the 42-label verified prefix from the registry loop artifact (the generic resolver returned empty) and confirmed level 2. Discovered the selection-ring/landing-preview oracle on the first probe clicks, learned the jump grammar, and walked a 6-jump chain -- then stranded the board (4 mutually unreachable survivors) and learned the auto-reset mechanic the hard way. Second attempt: full-map survey first (rail networks, two carriers including an inverted-U duct linking the west and east screens), then a planned 9-jump global line: drain the west rooms into a single rail-carried peg, clear all seven east pegs with a column cascade ending in a carrier-loading jump, ride the U-duct back west, and kill the isolated bottom-left pair from the carrier -- reduced to one peg and levels_completed hit 3. Self-ran the reproduction gate (146 labels, reached_level=3, reproduced=True). Continued into L4: solved its first three phases live (torch-hop chain across the entrance hall, carrier relay, east-wing vertical torch-ladder cascade and a two-peg merge into a third carrier) before reaching a dense central hub with at least two more pegs, a fourth carrier, and multi-screen geometry that needs systematic re-mapping; stopped there with ~160 steps left rather than risk a stranding reset that would burn the remaining budget -- L4 is clearly solvable with the documented mechanics and a fresh budget.",
+  "novel_mechanics_found": "L5 endgame is a TORCH-FERRY LOGISTICS puzzle on a closed rectangular rail network (top+bottom horizontal big rails joined by three vertical rails A/B/C spaced exactly one jump-pitch from the socket columns). The single east-network carrier ferries ONE movable torch; parking it on a vertical rail at a peg's row band (or on a horizontal big rail at a peg's column) creates a temporary crossable obstacle exactly between two socket cells, so a lone peg can traverse box-to-box in any direction. The win is a global reduce-to-one plan: 5 pegs (survivor from the west screen + 4 hub pegs) need 4 peg-over-peg removals, and each meeting point must be arranged so the JUMPER survives on a cell from which its next leg is reachable. Also confirmed: the west rail network and east (hub) rail network are permanently disconnected -- the west torch-carrier can never come east, so the endgame must be solved with the east carrier's torch plus the 4 fixed hub torches alone. L6 preview (scouted, not attempted): introduces a color-8 quad block that is ITSELF selectable and movable via the same jump mechanic -- it can jump down over an adjacent peg, and that peg can jump up over it (a second piece type, unknown whether its jumps remove crossed pegs); also a DOUBLE-socket carrier (two 4x4 sockets in one border), and no torches visible in the opening view.",
+  "narrative": "Resolved the 214-action verified prefix via exp4460's resolver (source: the codex L4 artifact), replayed it against a fresh reset, and confirmed levels_completed=4 at exactly step 214. On L5, instead of merging greedily (the prior session stranded all four hub pegs twice), I first executed the known forced west-screen merge (peg A double-torch-hops east, empty carrier descends the between-boxes rail stub, peg B jumps west over A into the carrier), rode the survivor east across two camera scrolls to the hub, took the one forced removal jump over the first hub peg, and then STOPPED and mapped the entire hub numerically (structure extraction over the raw grid: 4 pegs, 4 fixed torches, one torch-carrying carrier, 3 vertical + 2 horizontal rails). From that world model I derived a complete reduce-to-one line before touching anything: (1) ferry the carrier torch to rail A row-4 and rail B row-4 to walk my survivor into box E next to the E-peg, let the E-peg jump UP over it (removal, jumper survives on top); (2) park the torch on the top big rail to hop the survivor into the NE row as a sacrifice for the NE peg (removal, NE peg lands next to the fixed NE torch); (3) walk the NE peg west over the fixed torch, then down the c16 torch-ladder (top-rail torch park, box-M center torch, bottom-rail torch park, fixed SW torch) into the SE row, and let the SE peg take the final jump west over it. levels_completed hit 5 on that jump at live step 312 with zero resets and zero wasted jumps. Ran the self-verification gate: reproduce() from a fresh reset with the full 312-action sequence returned reached_level=5, reproduced=True. Spent 4 remaining clicks scouting L6 (found the movable color-8 block piece), stopping with budget to spare. NOTE: the previous content of this artifact file (the 2026-07-11 L3 line, cited by the registry's solver field) is preserved verbatim at results/outer_loop_fable5_lf52_probe_l3_20260711.json.",
